<p>Plugin4Shell is a security flaw in the plugin loading design of four AI coding agents: Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, GitHub Copilot and Gemini CLI. AIR Security disclosed the issue on September 17, 2026 after coordinated reporting to the vendors. The core problem is simple to describe: an agent can be told to use a specific Git commit, but fail to verify that the code actually checked out is that commit.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/plugin4shell-ai-coding-agent-security-2026.svg" width="1200" height="630" alt="Plugin4Shell supply-chain attack showing a trusted plugin, Git pin and malicious replacement"></figure>
<h2>What the vulnerability breaks</h2><p>Plugin marketplaces often pin a plugin to a reviewed commit. The idea is that the exact code a security team checked is the code the agent will run later. AIR Security found ways to make Git resolve a matching branch name instead of the intended commit in affected installation flows.</p><p>That makes the security boundary weaker than it looks. A marketplace can display the correct SHA while the local agent ends up executing different code. The issue is especially serious when plugins can run commands or read files with the user's permissions.</p>
<h2>Why it can become zero-click</h2><p>AIR Security says background plugin updates make the issue more dangerous. If an already-installed plugin is updated without another user approval, a repository takeover or malicious release can reach a developer machine without a new installation click.</p><p>This is a supply-chain problem, not simply a bad-plugin problem. A team can review the original plugin and still be exposed if the agent does not verify the final checked-out commit.</p>
<h2>Patch status is different across agents</h2><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>Status reported by AIR Security</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Claude Code</td><td>Patched in 2.1.179</td></tr><tr><td>OpenAI Codex</td><td>Patched in 0.146.0</td></tr><tr><td>GitHub Copilot</td><td>No fix reported by AIR Security at disclosure</td></tr><tr><td>Gemini CLI</td><td>Deprecated; AIR says Google will not patch it</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>What development teams should do</h2><ol><li>Update Claude Code and Codex to fixed versions or newer versions confirmed by the vendor.</li><li>Move away from deprecated Gemini CLI if it is still used for marketplace plugins.</li><li>Inventory installed plugins and their repositories.</li><li>Prefer trusted sources and reduce plugin permissions where the agent supports it.</li><li>For sensitive environments, disable automatic plugin updates until the vendor status is clear.</li><li>Review CI credentials and local tokens that an agent could reach.</li></ol>
<h2>The important engineering lesson</h2><p>Never treat a requested reference as proof of what was executed. The agent should resolve the final Git HEAD and compare it with the expected commit. If the values differ, installation should fail closed.</p><p>This principle applies beyond AI coding agents. Any system that downloads plugins, extensions or automation packages should verify the artifact that actually reaches execution.</p>
<h2>How this connects to wider AI-agent security</h2><p>Tool access is only one part of agent risk. The <a href="/ai-news/openai-claude-hack-security-lessons-2026">OpenAI security research involving Claude</a> shows how identity and third-party software can combine with AI-assisted security work. The safest approach is to treat the agent, its tools, its credentials and its external side effects as one system.</p>
<h2>What is not proven</h2><p>The public disclosure demonstrates a technical attack path. It does not prove that the flaw caused a widespread real-world campaign. Teams should separate a severe vulnerability from evidence of active exploitation.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is Plugin4Shell?</h3><p>It is a plugin supply-chain flaw that can bypass the intended protection of Git SHA pinning in four AI coding agents.</p><h3>Which agents are affected?</h3><p>AIR Security names Claude Code, Codex, GitHub Copilot and Gemini CLI.</p><h3>What is the safest first step?</h3><p>Update supported agents to vendor-fixed versions, audit installed plugins and reduce the credentials and files available to coding agents.</p>
